---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import ChunkOverlapDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/ChunkOverlapDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "15-chunking";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<SiteLayout title="15 · Chunking" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        A raw document is too big and too unstructured to embed as a single unit. Chunking splits it into smaller
        pieces that are each small enough to embed meaningfully and retrieve independently — but where you cut
        matters as much as how small the pieces are.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Fixed vs. recursive vs. semantic vs. layout-aware</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Four ways to decide where a chunk boundary falls:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">FIXED-SIZE</span>
        <p>Cuts every N characters/tokens regardless of content — simple, but slices sentences and tables in half.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">RECURSIVE</span>
        <p>Tries separators in order (paragraph, then sentence, then word) to prefer natural breaks within a size limit — the practical default.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">SEMANTIC</span>
        <p>Uses embedding similarity between adjacent sentences to find topic boundaries instead of a size limit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">LAYOUT-AWARE</span>
        <p>Uses the document's visual structure from Module 10's OCR/parsing stage, so a table row doesn't split from its header.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Chunk size and overlap</h2>
    <p>
      Too small loses the surrounding context a question needs ("orphaned context"). Too large dilutes the
      embedding with irrelevant text and wastes context-window budget at query time. <strong>Overlap</strong> —
      repeating the tail of one chunk at the start of the next — cuts the odds of a key sentence splitting at a
      boundary, at the cost of some storage and embedding redundancy.
    </p>

    <ChunkOverlapDemo />

    <h2>Parent-document retrieval: embed small, return big</h2>
    <p>
      Every strategy above uses one chunk size for both embedding and retrieval. <strong>Parent-document
      retrieval</strong> splits that in two: embed small "child" chunks (a sentence or two) for precise similarity
      search, but store a pointer from each child to its larger "parent" chunk or full section — and return the
      parent to the model, not the child. The small chunk wins the search; the bigger one answers the question.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sentence-window retrieval</strong> is the same idea at a smaller radius: match on a single sentence,
      then expand the returned context to the N sentences around it. Both patterns resolve the same tension as
      chunk size and overlap above — precise matching wants small chunks, useful answers want surrounding context —
      without forcing one size to do both jobs.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Picking one chunk size for every document type — a support ticket and a 200-page PDF don't want the same size.</li>
      <li>Zero overlap — a sentence that carries the answer gets cut exactly at a chunk boundary and neither half retrieves well.</li>
      <li>Chunking before parsing/OCR is done — you'll split mid-table or mid-figure-caption and lose the layout signal that would have prevented it.</li>
      <li>Never revisiting chunk size after looking at real retrieval failures — chunking strategy should be evaluated (Module 45), not set once and forgotten.</li>
      <li>Using the same chunk for both matching and answering — a size tuned for precise search is rarely the size that gives the model enough context to answer well.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</SiteLayout>
